/**
\class pdg::AnimatedAttributes
\ingroup Animation Graphics

\brief Drawing attributes with programmed movement and appearance animation.

AnimatedAttributes<> combines the Animated and Attributes interfaces. Construct it without a Drawing
or ElementRef; an optional Attributes argument seeds a snapshot of the initial values.
Animation tracks are not copied. Call animate(deltaSeconds) once per update; MVC controllers already
advance their Views. All durations, waits and update steps are floating-point seconds.
It has no physics body and does not animate stored geometry.

\section animated_attributes_channels Appearance channels

The changeLineColor(), changeLineThickness(), changeLineOpacity(),
changeFillColor(), changeFillOpacity(), changeFillGradient(),
changeFillRadialGradient(), changeRoundedCorners(), changeTextSize(),
changeSubsection(), changeSphereRotation(), changePolarOffset(),
changeLightOffset() and changeAmbientLight() methods take their target values,
an explicit duration in seconds and an optional EasingFunc (pdg::linearTween by default).
Sphere rotation also accepts an integer rotationDirection constant after easing.

blendMode, font, clipOverflow, lineStyle, textStyle, texture and fitType only have
immediate setters. No texture crossfade or interpolation of enum values is implied.
Gradient modes switch discretely when their delay expires; their numeric/color
values interpolate. Choosing a solid, linear or radial fill interrupts all previous
fill-color/gradient tracks, but leaves fill opacity and other channels alone.
Line/fill methods resolve lineStyle_Auto when requested, as Attributes does.

changeFrames(first, last, seconds, easing) visits an inclusive sequence of integer
frames in either direction, with equal time per frame before easing. It holds the
last frame at completion. A wait preserves the previous frame until the sequence
starts. Direct frame() calls interrupt the sequence.

Immediate setters interrupt their matching tracks. Independent channels continue.
wait(seconds) delays the next timed request, which begins from the value at the
end of the delay. Retargeting replaces a channel's pending request. Drawing and
ElementRef copy Attributes by default; use ElementRef::setLiveAttributes() to follow subsequent samples.

\section animated_attributes_transform Transform model

The initial logical size is 1 by 1, independent of Drawing geometry. Separate
x/y transform scale defaults to (1,1); scaling leaves logical size unchanged.
Animated movement, rotation, size and scale share one affine transform with the
appearance attributes. Its composition is translation, pivot, rotation,
shear, logical size, independent scale, inverse-pivot. The inherited Attributes
methods translation(), rotation(), scale(), skew() and transform() postmultiply
the current matrix; setTransform() replaces it. Matrix edits and matrix tweens
preserve logical width and height and update Animated position, rotation and
independent scale. Shear and singular matrices are retained. For example,
setScale(2) followed by scale(3) leaves getScale() at (6,6), without changing
logical size. grow() and stretch() change logical size while preserving scale.

Use the inherited Animated methods for position, rotation, size and scale:
moveTo() sets an absolute location, rotateTo() sets an absolute angle with optional
rotation direction, resizeTo() sets logical width/height, and changeScaleTo() sets
independent x/y scale. moveBy(), rotateBy(), resizeBy(), grow() and stretch() make
relative changes. Their timed overloads use easeInOutQuad by default; pass
linearTween explicitly for constant interpolation. setLocation(), setRotation(),
setSize() and setScale() apply immediate changes. Programmed movement, spin and
growth update the same matrix on each animate() step. These changes are visible
through getTransform() and in Attributes snapshots passed to Drawing.
A component request interrupts a whole-matrix tween at its current sample.
changeSkew() postmultiplies a shear onto the current matrix and interpolates to
that result. changeTransform() replaces the matrix by interpolating its six
affine coefficients. Such interpolation can pass through a singular matrix and
does not select an angular route: use rotateBy()/rotateTo() for rotations.
Matrices use column-major arrays of nine numbers in JavaScript and glm::mat3 in C++.

The Attributes composition calls are immediate drawing-transform operations.
translation() adds an offset along the current transformed axes; rotation() adds
a rotation around the supplied local center; scale() multiplies the current
transform around its supplied center. setLocation(), rotateTo() and changeScaleTo()
instead target individual Animated components. Both APIs keep the same matrix
and Animated state synchronized. Composition replaces transform tracks and stops
programmed transform rates at the current sample. Like other immediate edits,
translation(), rotation() and scale() reject a pending wait() or andThen().

Translation and axis scaling preserve existing rotation, signed scale and shear;
axis scaling multiplies the independent scale directly, even on collapsed axes.
General affine edits decompose matrix column lengths into independent scale
relative to the existing logical dimensions. A zero logical dimension can only
represent a zero corresponding matrix column; an incompatible matrix request
throws instead of changing that dimension. A queued matrix request is checked
again when it starts, after any preceding resize.

\section animated_attributes_types Language and Drawing integration

In C++, this class shares AnimatedBase and Attributes and can be passed as
either base reference. In JavaScript it inherits from Animated and
exposes the complete Attributes method surface. It is instanceof Animated and
instanceof AnimatedAttributes, but not instanceof Attributes; native Attributes
arguments in Port, Drawing and ElementRef accept it with the correct base-pointer
conversion. Copies of Attributes contain values, without animation tracks.

Views derive from AnimatedAttributes<View>. Transform, appearance and inherited
Attributes setters all return View&. Standalone AnimatedAttributes<> methods
return AnimatedAttributes<>&. Custom owners can derive from AnimatedAttributes<Owner>;
they share AnimatedAttributesBase and AnimatedBase for polymorphic access.
A further subclass of View retains View& as its chain return type.
Their size remains the layout rectangle's
width and height, and their location is its center. Passing a View directly as
Attributes therefore maps centered unit coordinates onto the view rectangle.
Existing ControlAttributes themes remain separate; inheriting this class does
not automatically replace every themed drawing primitive's attributes. General
MVC clipping/hit-testing for rotation and reflection remains pending.

\code{.cpp}
pdg::AnimatedAttributes<> style;
style.fillColor(pdg::Color(1.0f, 0.0f, 0.0f));
style.changeFillColor(pdg::Color(0.0f, 0.0f, 1.0f), 0.5, pdg::easeInOutQuad);
style.changeFillOpacity(0.25f, 0.5);
style.animate(0.25);
pdg::Attributes sample(style);  // value snapshot, without animation tracks
style.animate(0.25);            // sample retains its previous values
\endcode

*/
